00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  2020 was a year during which the country  reckoned with racial injustice  after the killing of george floyd calls  for police reform came from across the  country  including rethinking the role of police  in responding  to mental health crises lilia luciano  introduces us to a crisis response team  that's working  in partnership with police in eugene  oregon when you call 9-1-1 you don't  always get the police  if it's a mental health crisis you'll  get cahoots  for more than 30 years cahoots which  stands for crisis assistance helping out  on the streets  has been serving communities in central  oregon there's a guy on river road who i  think would benefit from  having cahoots come visit him responding  to a fifth of all 9-1-1  calls with unarmed crisis workers and  emts  instead of armed police officers we  respond in teams of two  to non-violent and non-criminal  crises so when people call 9-1-1  sometimes calls go to police  sometimes go to cahoots yes personally  having lost my father during a police  encounter it matters to me that  people feel as though there's an option  for them in the public safety system  that can support them through what  they're going through  crisis workers train for at least 500  hours on crisis intervention  for the police officers they partner  with that number  is closer to 40. i have a van full of  medical gear and clothes when we have it  and snacks i also can bring folks to a  crisis center  or somewhere to get a shower somewhere  to sober up for a little while so i have  a really large toolbox  when police show up they have the  hospital and they have the jail  more than a thousand people have been  shot and killed by police in the last  year  according to a washington post database  and one in four of those fatal police  shootings  involve a person with mental illness  according to the national alliance on  mental illness  angela kimball runs advocacy there  people are starting to rethink  a police response to mental health  crises  what do you take the concept of  defunding police to me  we take the concept of defunding police  to really be more about  investing in community resources that  really works to divert people from law  enforcement involvement  so we're talking about things like  crisis hotlines  mobile crisis response cahoots saves the  communities it serves an  estimated 22.5 million dollars annually  and local police say they welcome the  efforts now more than ever  i think the pandemic has caused a lot of  anxiety in the community  and a lot of the unknowns and people who  maybe initially didn't have glaring  mental health issues  those issues started to rise to the top  in the community and how do the police  officers  respond i mean do they feel threatened  like their job is  at risk just the opposite what cops want  to do is stop  crime catch criminal if a robbery  assault  burglary occurs we can respond to that  instead of being stuck on a call in  which we're  dealing with mental health issues he  says it lets cops be cops again  there's less burnout less overtime for  the agency and a safer community  for cbs this morning saturday luciano  eugene oregon so many things jump out on  that the hours of training  the need to work in conjunction with  each other and just the idea that you  have to rethink  how we are responding in communities  yeah the need to just think differently  yeah i remember this came up when i  covered daniel pruitt in rochester  because there were concerns about his  mental state he had been at a hospital  earlier  for that very reason and now you have  cities that are looking more seriously  at  how you can effectively police those  issues and then  you you saw on the report it even saves  money right

